        {"h2_html": "The call you miss is the inspection the agent gives to <em>the next name on the list</em>",
         "body_html": '<p style="color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.85;max-width:64ch">A buyer and their agent are usually working against an inspection-contingency deadline, the short window written into the contract when the inspection has to happen before the deal can move forward. That clock is why the call feels urgent, and it is why nobody on the other end is going to leave a voicemail and wait for a callback. If your line rings through, the agent just dials the next inspector on their list, and the one after that, until a real person picks up and puts it on the calendar. In this business the inspector who answers is very often the inspector who gets the job, and the agent who placed it.</p><p style="color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.85;max-width:64ch;margin-top:1rem">The hard part is that you usually cannot answer. During an inspection you are up in an attic tracing a furnace flue, folded into a crawlspace with no signal, or standing on a roof with a moisture meter in one hand, and that is exactly when the phone rings. So the call rolls to voicemail, the buyer and the agent move on, and an inspection you were fully able to handle goes to whoever happened to be free. An AI receptionist closes that gap. It answers on the first ring, stays calm and professional, finds out the property address, the closing or option date, and which agent is calling, and either books the inspection outright or texts it to you as a priority. The job is captured and scheduled instead of lost to the inspector who answered faster.</p>'},
